<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?><ns2:section xmlns:ns2="http://rest.cis.illinois.edu" id="41861" href="schedule/2014/spring/HIST/507/41861.xml"><parents><calendarYear id="2014" href="schedule/2014.xml">2014</calendarYear><term id="120141" href="schedule/2014/spring.xml">Spring 2014</term><subject id="HIST" href="schedule/2014/spring/HIST.xml">History</subject><course id="507" href="schedule/2014/spring/HIST/507.xml">Prob in Latin American Hist</course></parents><sectionNumber>A  </sectionNumber><statusCode>A</statusCode><sectionText>Topic:  Race Rebels in the Americas.  Description:  What and who is a "race rebel?"  And how, if at all, do class, gender, sexuality, and nationality shape our definition.  We explore these questions in multiple American contexts - from Portuguese and Spanish America to the Caribbean and the United States - and using a wide range of primary and secondary texts.  Students from all disciplines and specializing in any region are welcome.</sectionText><partOfTerm>1</partOfTerm><sectionStatusCode>A</sectionStatusCode><enrollmentStatus>UNKNOWN</enrollmentStatus><startDate>2014-01-21Z</startDate><endDate>2014-05-07Z</endDate><meetings><meeting id="0"><type code="DIS">Discussion/Recitation</type><start>01:00 PM</start><end>02:50 PM</end><daysOfTheWeek>R      </daysOfTheWeek><roomNumber>318</roomNumber><buildingName>Gregory Hall</buildingName><instructors><instructor lastName="Hertzman" firstName="M">Hertzman, M</instructor></instructors></meeting></meetings></ns2:section>